namespace Jazz2 {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page editor Level editor
@brief The level editor built into the game, and the formats it reads and writes

@tableofcontents

@section editor-overview Overview

The game has a built-in level editor, included by the `WITH_EDITOR` build option (see
@ref building-config-params-game). It's on by default on every platform that can save files, except the
<b>Nintendo 64</b>, <b>Dreamcast</b>, <b>GameCube</b> and <b>PlayStation 2</b>, and never in a dedicated server or
the Shareware Demo.

The editor works with the game's own level format. Original `.j2l` levels are converted on the first start (see
@ref paths) and can be opened from @cpp "Cache/" @ce.

@section editor-opening Opening the editor

-   <b>Level Editor</b> in the main menu opens an empty level and the <b>Open</b> dialog.
-   The `/edit` command-line switch opens a level by name (`episode/level`) or by path:

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1
Jazz2 /edit "C:\Levels\MyLevel.j2l"
@endcode

@section editor-interface Interface

-   <b>Menu bar and toolbar</b> at the top. The toolbar ends with the options of the current tool.
-   <b>Left panel</b> --- the tools, the current stamp, and the <b>Tiles</b> and <b>Events</b> tabs.
-   <b>Right panel</b> --- the layer list, with the properties of the selected layer or event below it.
-   <b>Status bar</b> --- how to use the current tool, the current layer, the tile and event under the pointer, and
    the zoom.

The level fills the window behind the panels.

-   <b>Panels</b> are resized by dragging their inner edge. Each panel can also float over the level: use the button
    in its header, or drag the header. Drag it back to its edge, or double-click the header, to dock it again.
-   <b>Small windows</b> --- below 600 units wide, the panels become drawers opened from the toolbar. Below 420 units,
    the tools move to a row at the bottom. The <b>View</b> menu can fix the layout to desktop or compact.
-   <b>Expert Mode</b> (<b>View</b> menu, on by default) shows all tools and commands. Turned off, only the basic ones
    are shown.
-   <b>Interface size</b> follows the window size and the display scaling of the system (its DPI), so it isn't tiny on
    a small screen with many pixels, such as a phone. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>+</kbd> / <kbd>-</kbd>
    makes it larger or smaller, in steps of 25% from 100% to 600%.
-   <b>Safe Area</b> (<b>Options</b> > <b>Graphics</b> in the game) also applies to the editor. The panels, windows and
    the bar shown while playing keep clear of the cut-off edges. While editing, the edges are filled like the bars.
-   The tools on the left scroll with the wheel or a finger when the window is too short for them.

In dialogs, <kbd>Enter</kbd> chooses the highlighted button. <kbd>Esc</kbd> or the cross closes the dialog without
choosing anything. The cross can also be hit anywhere in the corner of the window. Windows such as
<b>Check Level</b>, <b>Statistics</b>, the <b>Navigator</b> or the <b>Palette</b> stay open beside the level until
they're closed. Opening one again brings it to the front, and its shortcut closes it. Some windows, such as
<b>History</b>, the <b>Navigator</b> and the <b>Event List</b>, can be resized by their edges. A smaller Navigator
shortens the list of bookmarks first, then the map. Every color has a swatch that opens a color picker.

@subsection editor-interface-keyboard Using the interface with the keyboard

-   <kbd>Tab</kbd> and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> move between controls. <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd>
    presses the focused one, <kbd>Esc</kbd> gives the keys back to the level.
-   A dropdown opens with <kbd>Enter</kbd>, <kbd>Space</kbd>, <kbd>F4</kbd> or <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Down</kbd>.
    <kbd>Up</kbd> and <kbd>Down</kbd> change it without opening it.
-   <kbd>Alt</kbd> with the underlined letter opens a menu. In menus and long lists, typing a letter jumps to the next
    item that starts with it.
-   Text fields have their own undo (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd>), and
    <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with the arrows, <kbd>Backspace</kbd> or <kbd>Del</kbd> moves or deletes by words.
-   On macOS, <kbd>Cmd</kbd> works as <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>.

@subsection editor-interface-accessibility Contrast and motion

In the <b>View</b> menu:

-   <b>High Contrast Interface</b> --- a theme with stronger contrast.
-   <b>Reduce Motion</b> --- nothing animates: panels and lists don't slide, the view jumps instead of flying, and a
    play-test starts without the growing ring, with the music opened up and muffled again at once.

@subsection editor-interface-navigator Navigator, bookmarks and notes

The <b>Navigator</b> (<kbd>F2</kbd>) shows the whole level in small. Click or drag in it to move the view.
<b>Events</b> adds the events and the camera limits. Below the map:

-   <b>Bookmarks</b> 1 to 9 --- set at the view center by <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> --
    <kbd>9</kbd>, gone to by <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd>. Each can be renamed and given a color.
-   <b>Notes</b> --- texts placed in the level (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd> at the pointer). A note
    keeps its author and time and can be replied to, so notes work as review threads. <b>Done</b> marks a note as
    resolved.

Bookmarks and notes can be undone like any other change, and export leaves them out.

@section editor-controls Controls

@subsection editor-controls-mouse Mouse

-   <b>Left button</b> --- use the tool. <b>Right button</b> --- pick the tile under the pointer (brush), or erase
    (rectangle, fill, events).
-   <b>Wheel</b> --- zoom at the pointer. With <kbd>Shift</kbd> it scrolls sideways, with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> up and down,
    with <kbd>Alt</kbd> it changes the layer, and with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd> the interface size.
-   <b>Middle button</b>, or the left one with <kbd>Space</kbd> held --- pan the view.
-   <b>Number fields</b> --- drag, click to type, or use the wheel (<kbd>Shift</kbd> for steps of ten). A drag is one
    undo step.
-   <b>Dropdowns</b> --- the wheel steps through the choices.

Zoomed out below 15 %, layers are drawn from cached images (@ref Editor::TileLod), so even a large level stays fast.
The images are as sharp as the screen allows, up to 16 pixels per tile. Palette-shifted, solid and effect layers are
always drawn as tiles.

@subsection editor-controls-keyboard Keyboard

Keys | Action
---- | ------
<kbd>B</kbd>, <kbd>E</kbd>, <kbd>R</kbd>, <kbd>G</kbd>, <kbd>I</kbd> | Brush, eraser, rectangle, fill, eyedropper
<kbd>M</kbd>, <kbd>W</kbd>, <kbd>Q</kbd>, <kbd>H</kbd> | Select, magic wand, events, hand
<kbd>T</kbd>, <kbd>P</kbd>, <kbd>U</kbd>, <kbd>K</kbd>, <kbd>L</kbd> | Smart brush, ground line, measure, events along path, shapes
<kbd>Space</kbd> / <kbd>Alt</kbd> (held) | Hand / eyedropper while held
<kbd>X</kbd> / <kbd>Y</kbd> | Flip the stamp horizontally / vertically
<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> | Turn the stamp clockwise
<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> | Paint random tiles of the stamp / reroll them
<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Edit a layer. With <kbd>Shift</kbd>, hide or show it
<kbd>Page Up</kbd> / <kbd>Page Down</kbd> | Previous / next layer
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> | Undo / redo (also <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>)
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd>, <kbd>C</kbd>, <kbd>V</kbd> | Cut, copy, paste --- pasted tiles become the stamp
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> / <kbd>V</kbd> | Copy / paste all layers with their events
<kbd>Del</kbd> | Delete the selection
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> / <kbd>D</kbd> / <kbd>I</kbd> | Select all / deselect / invert --- the tiles, or the events while the event tool is chosen
<kbd>Shift</kbd>+arrows | Move the selected tiles
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd> / <kbd>Y</kbd> | Mirror the selection horizontally / vertically
<kbd>Esc</kbd> | Cancel the current action, then deselect
<kbd>+</kbd>, <kbd>-</kbd> / <kbd>0</kbd> / <kbd>Home</kbd> | Zoom in, out / zoom as in the game / fit the whole level
Arrows | Move the view
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Grid / flat layers
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> | Pause layers that move by themselves
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Show events while using other tools
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> | Collision masks: over the tiles, instead of them, off
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> | Show where the player can get
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> | Level texts
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> | Find and replace
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd> | Continue the row right of the pointer with suggested tiles
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd>, <kbd>O</kbd>, <kbd>S</kbd> | New, open, save
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> | Save as / export
<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd> | Play, play here, play from start --- <kbd>F5</kbd> while playing goes back to the editor
<kbd>F6</kbd>, <kbd>F7</kbd>, <kbd>F8</kbd>, <kbd>F9</kbd> (while playing) | Move the player to the pointer, stop time, next frame, slow motion --- see @ref editor-playing-debug
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd> | Path of the last play-test
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> -- <kbd>9</kbd> | Set a bookmark / go to it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd> | Add a note at the pointer
<kbd>F2</kbd> / <kbd>F3</kbd> | Navigator / event list
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> | History --- click a change to go back or forward to it
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> | Command palette --- search all commands by name
<kbd>F1</kbd> | Keyboard shortcuts

@subsection editor-controls-shortcuts Changing shortcuts

In <b>Keyboard Shortcuts</b> (<kbd>F1</kbd>), click a shortcut and press the new keys. <kbd>Backspace</kbd> removes
it, <kbd>Esc</kbd> keeps it. An action that had the same keys loses them. Actions can be searched by name or by keys
(`ctrl+s`), and <b>Press Keys</b> finds what a shortcut does. <b>Reset All</b> restores the defaults.

The side buttons of a mouse have no use of their own, so they can be shortcuts too, with or without modifiers.
Press one instead of the keys, it's shown as `Mouse 4` or `Mouse 5`.

<b>Shortcuts like</b> applies a preset (@ref Editor::KeymapPreset):

-   <b>This Editor</b> --- the defaults.
-   <b>Jazz Creation Station</b> --- <kbd>F</kbd> and <kbd>I</kbd> flip the stamp (<kbd>Alt</kbd> still picks a
    tile).
-   <b>MLLE</b> --- as Jazz Creation Station, and <kbd>R</kbd> turns the stamp, so the rectangle moves to
    <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>.

Some shortcuts work only while the level is played --- <b>Back to Editor</b> and the debug tools --- which is why
<kbd>F5</kbd> both starts and stops a play-test.

Changed shortcuts are saved in @cpp "Jazz2.EditorHotkeys.json" @ce in the configuration directory, as an object of
action IDs and their shortcuts, such as @cpp "Ctrl+Shift+Z" @ce or @cpp "Mouse 4" @ce, or @cpp null @ce for none. Only those that differ
from the defaults are written. <b>Export</b> and <b>Import</b> move them to another computer.

@subsection editor-controls-gamepad Gamepad

Pressing a gamepad button shows a cursor and lists the buttons in the status bar. Using the mouse or the keyboard
switches back.

Gamepad | Action
------- | ------
Left stick | Move the cursor, faster the further and longer it's pushed
Directional pad | Move the cursor by one tile. Over a number, change it
Right stick | Move the view. Over a list, scroll it
Left / right trigger | Zoom out / in at the cursor
A | Use the tool, or press what the cursor is over
B | Erase, or close what's open over the interface
X | Pick the tile or event under the cursor
Y | Next tool. Hold it for a tool ring (aim with the left stick, release to pick). Over a text field, open a keyboard
Left / right bumper | Previous / next layer
Back / left stick press | Undo / redo
Start | Play

While a dialog waits for an answer, the gamepad only moves the cursor and presses A and B. Text is typed with the
system's on-screen keyboard where there is one, otherwise with the editor's own.

@subsection editor-controls-touch Touch screen

The status bar lists the gestures.

Fingers | Action
------- | ------
One | Use the tool once the finger moves a little. A quick tap is a click. Over the interface, it acts as a pointer
Two | Pan, and pinch to zoom
Tap with two / three | Undo / redo
Hold one still | Tool ring, tap to pick
Tap a text field | System keyboard, or the editor's own

Over the interface, a finger held still shows the tooltip, and lists scroll by dragging them. In the layer list and
the tile picker, hold a finger still for half a second before dragging: then it moves the layer or picks a block of
tiles, instead of scrolling. A number field opens the keyboard when tapped, and changes the number when dragged.

While a level is played, a finger on the bar at the top uses the bar, the other fingers play the game.

@section editor-layers Layers

The layer list is in the right panel:

-   The <b>eye</b> hides a layer. <kbd>Alt</kbd>+click on it shows only that layer, or all layers again. The
    <b>lock</b> protects a layer from changes. Both only affect the editor, not the level.
-   Drag a layer to reorder it, double-click it to rename it. An unnamed layer is named by what it is, such as
    <em>Foreground</em> or <em>Background</em>. Scripts can find a layer by its name, the names a level made in MLLE
    gives its layers are kept. A layer of a level of the original game also keeps its number there, 1 to 8, which
    scripts of JJ2+ refer to it by wherever it's moved; a duplicate of it doesn't get the number.
-   <b>Duplicate Layer</b> and <b>Resize Layer</b> are in the <b>Layer</b> menu. Removing a layer asks first.

@subsection editor-layers-properties Layer properties

The <b>speed</b>, <b>offset</b> and <b>model</b> of each axis decide where the layer is, as in JJ2+:

Model | Where the layer is | JJ2+
----- | ------------------ | ----
<b>Speed</b> | Moves by its speed and automatic speed. It meets the top left of the level only at speed 1 | `LAYERSPEEDMODEL::BOTHSPEEDS`
<b>Speed from level start</b> | The same, but starts at the top left of the level in a view of any size | `LAYERSPEEDMODEL::FROMSTART`
<b>Fixed to screen</b> | Ignores its speeds and offset | `LAYERSPEEDMODEL::LAYER8`
<b>Fit to level</b> | Spans the whole level, whatever its speeds | ---
<b>Speed of view size</b> | The speed is its position with the view at the top left of the level, the automatic speed at the bottom right, in sizes of the view | ---

<b>Inherent offset</b> (`limitVisibleRegion` of JJ2+) moves a layer of <b>Speed</b> that doesn't repeat vertically
down by half of how much taller the view is than 200 pixels.

-   <b>Movement</b> presets set the speeds and models for common kinds of layers. <em>Custom</em> means none of them
    matches, as in most converted levels.
-   <b>Renderer</b> --- how the layer is drawn, see @ref editor-layers-renderers.
-   <b>Color</b> --- what it means depends on the renderer: a multiplier of the tiles, the tint, or the color of the
    horizon.
-   <b>Editor opacity</b> --- how see-through the editor shows the layer, to see the layers behind it. Editor-only, the
    game doesn't use it.

<b>Resize Layer</b> changes the size around an anchor point. The new area is empty, extends the edge tiles, or repeats
the layer. Events, the water level, bookmarks and notes move with the sprite layer. With the <b>Select</b> or
<b>Hand</b> tool, the frame of the layer also has handles to resize it by dragging.

@subsection editor-layers-renderers Renderers

Renderer | What it draws
-------- | -------------
<b>Tiles</b> | The tiles as they are
<b>Solid color</b> | The color of the layer where its tiles are
<b>Tinted</b> | The tiles tinted by a palette entry
<b>Palette shift</b> | Each pixel shows the color <b>Shift</b> entries further in the palette, as `SPRITE::PALSHIFT` of JJ2+. <b>Opacity</b> sets how opaque the layer is. True color tile sets aren't shifted
<b>Single color</b> | Only the shapes of the tiles, every visible pixel in the palette entry <b>Color</b>, such as a shadow of another layer, as `SPRITE::SINGLECOLOR` of JJ2+. <b>Opacity</b> sets how opaque the layer is
<b>X-ray</b> | The last frame of each animated tile only where an object is behind it, as its silhouette
<b>Blur behind</b> | Not its tiles, but what is behind them, moved, mirrored or blurred by the colors of the tiles
<b>Chroma key</b> | The tiles only where what is behind them has the color of the palette entry <b>Key</b>, so an area painted in that color is a window the layer shows through, as `SPRITE::CHROMAKEY` of JJ2+. <b>Opacity</b> sets how opaque the layer is. Backends that can't read what is behind a layer don't draw it
<b>Sky</b> | The texture as two planes stretching to the horizon, as `TEXTURE::WARPHORIZON` of JJ2+
<b>Tunnel</b> | The texture as circles getting smaller into the distance, as `TEXTURE::TUNNEL`
<b>Wave</b> | The texture where the tiles would be, its rows and columns moved by sine waves, as `TEXTURE::WAVE`
<b>Cylinder</b> | The texture as the inside of a horizontal tube, as `TEXTURE::CYLINDER`
<b>Reflection</b> | What is behind it, mirrored below a horizon and rippled by the texture, as `TEXTURE::REFLECTION`

The sprite layer can only be drawn as tiles, in a solid color, tinted, with a palette shift, or in a single color.

-   <b>Texture</b> of a sky, tunnel, wave, cylinder or reflection --- <em>Its tiles</em> (the 8×8 tiles at the top left of
    the layer), or an <em>Own image</em> of 256×256 pixels stored in the level and shown under the row. A level
    converted from JJ2+ gets an own image where it used a JJ2+ texture or an image made in MLLE. The menu of the row
    imports a PNG (its colors are matched to the palette), exports the image to edit it elsewhere, turns the tiles into
    an own image, or goes back to the tiles.
-   <b>Sky</b> --- the <b>Vanishing point</b> and the <b>Horizon line</b> in percent of the view, and <b>Fade</b> into
    the color of the horizon.
-   <b>Area</b> of a sky, tunnel or cylinder --- the <em>Whole view, Resurrection</em> as this game drew sky layers
    before version 4.0, the <em>Whole view, JJ2+</em> in the perspective of JJ2+ with the texture placed by the speeds of the
    layer (`SURFACE::FULLSCREEN`), or only where the layer is, as a window (`SURFACE::INNERWINDOW`) or as part of the
    layer (`SURFACE::INNERLAYER`). The texture then moves by <b>Inner speed</b> and <b>Inner auto</b> inside the layer.
    JJ2+ draws the sky layer of a level saved by JCS (`SURFACE::LEGACY`) in its perspective too, placed where layer 5
    is by the speed model of layer 8, so a converted level of JJ2+ (with MLLE data or a script) gets <em>Whole view,
    JJ2+</em> and any other level <em>Whole view, Resurrection</em>. A cylinder is always drawn in the perspective of
    JJ2+, so it has only one <em>Whole view</em>.
-   <b>Tunnel</b> --- <b>Center</b> in percent of the view, <b>Fade</b> into the color of the horizon, and
    <b>Spiral</b>.
-   <b>Wave</b> --- the <b>Wavelength</b> of the waves that move the rows left and right and of those that move the
    columns up and down (one more pixel than the number, 0 for none), their <b>Amplitude</b> (1 moves the texture by
    128 pixels each way), their <b>Speed</b> along the texture, and <b>Swap angles</b> to move the vertical waves the
    other way. The waves follow the texture, as in JJ2+. Backends without shaders draw its tiles, without the waves.
-   <b>Cylinder</b> --- <b>Center</b> of the horizon, <b>Fade</b> of the far wall, and <b>Half size</b> for a more
    curved tube.
-   <b>Reflection</b> --- below the <b>Horizon line</b>, mirrors what is behind it as on water. The texture ripples the
    reflection towards the <b>Vanishing point</b>: <b>Distortion</b> sets how strongly, <b>Distance</b> how small the
    ripples are. <b>Tint</b> and <b>Tint opacity</b> color it, <b>Blur</b> blurs it horizontally. The <b>Area</b> is
    the whole view, or only where the layer is, as a window (`SURFACE::INNERWINDOW`) or as part of the layer
    (`SURFACE::INNERLAYER`). The ripples then move by <b>Inner speed</b> and <b>Inner auto</b> instead of the speeds of
    the layer. Only the last reflection layer is drawn, and only on backends that support it.

While a layer is selected, a sky, tunnel, wave, cylinder or reflection is shown as plain tiles so it can be painted --- except
a reflection with its own image, which keeps rippling. <b>Show Textured Layers</b> in the View menu (Ctrl+Shift+T), or
<b>Show as in game</b> in the properties of the layer, draws it as in the game instead, to see what its properties do.

Layers converted from MLLE keep their sprite mode: `PALSHIFT` and `TRANSLUCENTPALSHIFT` become a palette shift,
`TINTED` a tinted layer, `SINGLECOLOR` and `TRANSLUCENTCOLOR` a single color layer (the latter with an opacity of
192), `CHROMAKEY` a chroma key layer, and `TRANSLUCENT` and `INVISIBLE` ordinary tiles with an alpha of 192 or 0 (an
invisible layer is still solid).

@subsection editor-layers-view How layers are shown

-   Layers scroll with parallax, as the game would place them for a player at the view center. <b>Flat Layers</b>
    (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) aligns them all to one grid, which helps when editing backgrounds.
-   A layer that moves by itself stays still while it's selected. <b>Pause Layer Movement</b>
    (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) stops all of them.
-   The selected layer is framed in white, and darkened outside where it doesn't repeat. A repeating layer shows
    faint lines where it repeats, and the frame is around the copy nearest the middle of the view.
-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> shows the collision masks of the selected layer. On the sprite layer, symbols show
    what events make the tiles do --- one-way, vine, hook, pole, hurting, destructible and so on. Translucent tiles
    are tinted, invisible ones crossed out.

@section editor-level Level properties

In the <b>Level</b> menu:

-   <b>Properties</b> --- name, author, next, secret and bonus levels, music, starting light, weather, water level,
    the caption tile, and the pit and multiplayer settings.
    -   The <b>author</b> defaults to the player name from the options.
    -   The <b>caption tile</b> is shown in the level list. <b>Auto</b> picks a colorful tile the level uses a lot.
    -   Whether the level is a race or has Capture the Flag isn't chosen here. It follows from the events when the
        level is saved: a warp with <b>Set Lap</b> makes a race, a CTF base makes Capture the Flag.
-   <b>Texts</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>) --- events refer to texts by number, so new texts are added at the end
    and only the last one can be removed. The arrows reorder texts, and the events using them follow.
    <kbd>Enter</kbd> starts a new line, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms.
-   <b>Episode</b> --- see @ref editor-level-episode.

Saving also stores a small preview of the start of the level, which the custom level list shows.

@subsection editor-level-translations Translating level texts

In <b>Level Texts</b>, choose a language at the top, or <b>Add Language</b> by its code (`de`, `pt-br`), and type
the translations next to the texts. An empty translation shows the original text. The game shows the translation to
its language, or to the language without its region (`pt` for `pt-br`).

For translators, <b>Export .pot</b> writes a gettext template, <b>Export .po</b> a catalog with the translations of
the chosen language, and <b>Import .po</b> reads one back.

@subsection editor-level-episode Episode

<b>Episode</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu shows the episode of the level --- the folder it's in and its `.j2e` file:

-   the name, the first level, the episodes before and after it, and its position in the list (the original episodes
    are below 100),
-   the title and background images, which can be imported and exported as PNG,
-   the levels in the order their links lead, with broken links and levels nothing leads to marked.

<b>Rename</b> renames a level and its script, and updates the links to it in other levels. The previous files are kept
as `.bak`.

Levels in `unknown` belong to no episode. For them, the editor offers to create a <b>New Episode</b> with the level as
its first, to <b>Move to Episode</b>, or to list the <b>Levels of No Episode</b>. Moving lists every change first. The
level is then saved into the folder of the episode, with its script and unsaved changes, and removed from `unknown`,
and links to it in other levels are updated (their previous files kept as `.bak`).

@section editor-tiles Tiles

@subsection editor-tiles-picker Tile picker

-   Click picks a tile, drag picks a rectangle. The arrows next to the name switch between tile sets and animated
    tiles.
-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+wheel or the buttons on the right change the tiles per row, 10 by default.
-   With the brush, the <b>Next</b> row suggests tiles that other levels put next to the tiles around the pointer.
    <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd> fills the row right of the pointer with them.
-   The magnifier button in the header shows how much each tile is used.
-   The strip above the tiles holds favourite and recent tiles. Right-click a tile to make it a favourite.

@subsection editor-tiles-edit Editing a tile

Double-click a tile in the picker, or use <b>Edit Tile of the Brush</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu, to change its pixels
and collision mask. The change applies only to this level, the tile set file stays as it is.

-   Tools: pencil, line, rectangle, fill, <b>Replace</b> (recolors every pixel of a color), selection and eyedropper.
    Left paints, right erases.
-   Buttons below flip, rotate and shift the whole tile. The mirror buttons make painting mirrored. Copy and paste move
    pixels between tiles.
-   <b>Seams</b> shows the tile repeated around itself, to check that it tiles.
-   <b>Paint in Level</b> paints right in the level, everywhere the layer shows the tile.
-   <b>True Color</b> paints any color. The tile is first copied into the tiles of this level.
-   Mask: <b>Mask from Pixels</b>, <b>Fill Below Surface</b>, <b>Slope</b>, <b>Mask of Brush Tile</b>, <b>Empty</b>
    and <b>Solid</b>.
-   <b>As in Tile Set</b> drops the changes. <b>Export PNG</b> and <b>Import PNG</b> exchange the tile with another
    program.
-   An animated tile shows its frames, each edited as its own tile.

8-bit tiles keep palette indices, so a color is reduced to the nearest one of the palette --- except in the tiles of
this level.

@subsection editor-tiles-level Tiles of this level

The <b>tiles of this level</b> are an extra tile set stored inside the level, created when tiles are first added to it.

-   Its tiles are true color, with transparency.
-   <b>Copy into This Level</b> in the window of any tile copies the tile there, and the level uses the copy instead.
    That way any tile can get true color changes in a single level.
-   <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b> moves them into a tile set file.

@subsection editor-tiles-animated Animated tiles

<b>Animated Tiles</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu lists them.

-   A new animated tile is made of the brush tiles. <b>Add Brush</b> adds them as frames.
-   Frame time is in game frames (60 per second). The delay holds the last frame longer, and an animation can play
    forward and back.
-   A removed animated tile is replaced by its first frame everywhere. <b>Paint with It</b> makes it the brush.

@subsection editor-tiles-sets Tile sets

All in the <b>Level</b> menu. New and changed tile set files are saved into the user content as
`Tilesets/<name>.j2t`.

Command | What it does
------- | ------------
<b>Tile Sets</b> | The default tile set and the extra ones. An extra one can use a range of its file and have its colors matched to the level palette. The tiles of the level are remapped, so they keep their look
<b>New Tile Set</b> | Empty tiles in the level palette
<b>New Tile Set from Image</b> | Cuts an image into 32×32 tiles, with the options below
<b>Add Tiles from Image</b> | Adds the tiles of an image to a tile set of the level, a new tile set, or the tiles of this level
<b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b> | Moves the tile changes of this level into the tile set file (the previous one kept as `.bak`), or into a new tile set
<b>Rearrange Tile Set</b> | Deletes, moves and inserts tiles, merges duplicates and removes unused ones, see below
<b>Export Tile Set Image</b> | Saves the tile set as PNG, 10 tiles per row, with the masks in `.mask.png`
<b>Generate Effect Tiles</b> | Tiles for a <b>Blur behind</b> layer: glass, glass blocks, frosted glass, water, heat haze, a magnifier or a puddle

Options of the tiles from an image:

-   <b>Colors</b> --- keep the palette of the image, keep true color, map to the level palette, or generate a palette
    (it keeps the entries the sprites use).
-   <b>Dither</b> --- by an ordered pattern, or by error diffusion.
-   <b>Margin</b> and <b>Gutters</b> for sprite sheets, a <b>Transparent</b> color, and the <b>Alpha</b> a pixel needs
    to be seen.
-   <b>Skip tiles that are the same as another</b>.
-   A `.mask.png` next to the image gives the masks. Otherwise the tiles are solid where they're opaque. An image from
    <b>Export Tile Set Image</b> comes back unchanged.

In <b>Rearrange Tile Set</b>, click, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click selects tiles, and dragging moves
them. Levels refer to tiles by their position, so it either saves the tile set as itself and updates the levels that
use it --- the previous files kept as `.bak`, <b>Files</b> listing them first and <b>Restore Backups</b> putting them
back, and this clears the undo history --- or saves a new tile set that only this level uses.

@subsection editor-tiles-palette Palette

<b>Palette</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu shows the palette in use. Only the level's own palette can be changed. It starts
as a copy of the tile set's.

-   Click picks an entry, drag or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click a range. Edit it as RGB, HSV, `#RRGGBB` or with the color
    picker. The level, sprites included, updates live.
-   <b>Gradient</b> fills a range between its ends. <b>From Tile Set</b> restores the colors of the tile set.
-   <b>Keep the colors of sprites</b> protects the entries that sprites use.
-   <b>Tiles Using It</b> makes the tiles that use the entry the brush.
-   The arrows move entries, <b>Swap</b> swaps two. <b>Keep the look of the level</b> remaps the tiles so the level
    looks the same.
-   <b>Mark</b> crosses out the unused entries, those the sprites use, or those that differ from the tile set.
-   <b>Into Tile Set</b> makes it the palette of the tile set. <b>Import</b> and <b>Export</b> read and write JASC
    (`.pal`), GIMP (`.gpl`), Adobe (`.act`) or PNG.

@section editor-tools Painting and selecting

Choose a tool on the left, or in the <b>Tools</b> menu. Its options are at the end of the toolbar.

Tool | How it works | Options
---- | ------------ | -------
<b>Brush</b> (<kbd>B</kbd>) | Paints the stamp, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+drag paints a line | Stamp options
<b>Eraser</b> (<kbd>E</kbd>) | Erases, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+drag erases a line | ---
<b>Rectangle</b> (<kbd>R</kbd>) | Fills a rectangle with the stamp | Stamp options, <b>Only empty</b>
<b>Fill</b> (<kbd>G</kbd>) | Fills the area of the same tile | Stamp options, <b>Only empty</b>, <b>Across edges</b>, <b>Flipped too</b>
<b>Shapes</b> (<kbd>L</kbd>) | Draws a line, rectangle, ellipse or filled ellipse. <kbd>Shift</kbd> keeps lines at 45° and shapes square | The shape, stamp options, <b>Only empty</b>
<b>Select</b> (<kbd>M</kbd>) | Selects a rectangle of tiles | ---
<b>Magic Wand</b> (<kbd>W</kbd>) | Selects joined tiles like the clicked one, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click all of them in the layer | <b>Whole layer</b>, <b>Across edges</b>, <b>Flipped too</b>
<b>Eyedropper</b> (<kbd>I</kbd>) | Picks a tile | ---
<b>Hand</b> (<kbd>H</kbd>) | Moves the view | ---

<b>Across edges</b> goes on at the other side of a repeating layer, <b>Flipped too</b> counts a flipped tile as the
same one. The stamp options are:

-   <b>Flip</b> (<kbd>X</kbd>, <kbd>Y</kbd>) and <b>turn</b> (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>) the stamp. Turning moves
    the tiles around, but the tiles themselves can only be flipped.
-   <b>Random tiles</b> (<kbd>Z</kbd>) paints a random tile of the stamp in each cell --- the same one each time it's
    painted over, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> rerolls --- optionally flipped at random. <b>Weights of Tiles</b> sets
    how often each tile comes up.
-   <b>Tiles</b> --- paint the tiles <b>As they are</b>, or <b>Normal</b>, <b>Translucent</b> or <b>Invisible</b>, as
    the tile modifiers of the original game, or with <b>Heat</b>, the heat effect of JJ2+: every row of pixels of the
    tile wavers left and right, as if seen through heat (only in a layer drawn as tiles, and only on backends with
    shaders). <b>Only modifier</b> changes only the modifier.

The <b>Select</b> menu:

-   <b>Invert Selection</b>, <b>Grow</b>, <b>Shrink</b> and <b>Select All Tiles Like These</b>.
-   Selected tiles are moved by dragging them, or by <kbd>Shift</kbd>+arrows.
-   <b>Mirror Selection</b> flips the tiles and, on the sprite layer, the events on them, with their parameters changed
    as they should be --- springs, directions, offsets. <b>Mirroring Swaps Teams of Events</b> also swaps the first two
    teams, for symmetric multiplayer levels.
-   <b>Copy All Layers</b> and <b>Paste All Layers</b> copy the area of every visible, unlocked layer with its events.
    Pasted warps and triggers get new identifiers.

Copied tiles and events also go to the system clipboard as text, so they can be pasted into another level, or into the
game running elsewhere.

@subsection editor-tools-find Find and Replace

<b>Find and Replace</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>):

-   <b>Tiles</b> --- finds the brush tile, flipped the same way or any way, in the current layer, in all layers, or only
    in the selection. <b>Select</b> selects them, <b>Replace</b> puts another tile in, keeping its flips and
    translucency.
-   <b>Events</b> --- finds events of a kind, optionally only those with the values of the parameters ticked, and
    replaces them with another kind. The parameters are edited as in the event properties. The new events get the
    values set for them. With <b>Keep matching parameters</b>, they keep the values of parameters of the same name,
    which are dimmed then. <b>Selected</b> takes the kind and the parameters of the selected event.

Replacing events asks first. Each replacement is one undo step.

@section editor-terrain Terrain tools

-   <b>Smart Brush</b> (<kbd>T</kbd>) --- the left button paints ground, the right one removes it. Each tile is chosen
    by what other levels of the tile set put there with the same neighbors, so blocks get their tops, edges and
    corners automatically. The levels are read the first time it's used (the status bar shows the progress) and cached
    in `Cache/Editor/Adjacency/`.
-   <b>Choose Tiles by Their Neighbors</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) picks the selected tiles again the same way. <b>Fill
    Selection with Ground</b> fills the selection with ground.
-   <b>Beautify Selection</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) replaces the selected tiles with ones that fit their neighbors better
    and have the same collision mask, so the collision stays exactly the same.

@subsection editor-terrain-rules Terrain rules and prefabs

<b>Terrain Rules</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu shows the tile used for each of the 47 ways the surrounding cells can be
solid, and lets you override it:

-   Click assigns the brush tiles --- several of them alternate. Right-click restores the learned tile. The overrides
    are used first by the Smart Brush and the Ground Line tool.
-   A tile set can have several <b>terrains</b>, such as ground, wall or cloud.
-   <b>Slopes</b> tags a tile as a slope with heights at both edges, or excludes it from ground.
-   <b>Try It</b> is a small sandbox. <b>Coverage</b> shows which of the 47 still need a rule, in red.

<b>Prefabs</b> in the <b>Edit</b> menu keeps named stamps for a tile set: <b>Save Brush</b> saves one, <b>Use</b>
makes it the brush. <b>Save Copy of All Layers</b> keeps a copy of all layers with events, such as a warp pair or a
base for Capture the Flag.

Rules and prefabs are stored in `Tilesets/<name>.rules`. The game includes rules for `castle1`, `diam1` and `jungle2`.

@subsection editor-terrain-ground-line Ground Line tool

The <b>Ground Line</b> tool (<kbd>P</kbd>) lays ground along a line. Click to add points (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> snaps to
tile corners), right-click to lay it.

-   The tiles along the line get the ground pieces whose masks follow it best, filled down to existing ground.
-   <b>Lays</b> --- <b>Ground</b> below the line, <b>Ceiling</b> above it, a <b>Band</b> as thick as the
    <b>Depth</b>, or a closed <b>Shape</b>. <b>Smooth</b> makes it a curve.
-   The line stays editable: drag a point, click the line to add one, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click to remove one, right-click
    the line to remove its ground. Tiles edited by hand since are left alone.
-   Its collision surface is drawn over it, green where it matches, red where it misses.
-   <b>Make Tiles Along Ground Line</b> (<b>Edit</b> menu) makes tiles of this level so the collision follows the line
    exactly. <b>Make Ground Line from Selection</b> traces existing ground, so it can be reshaped.

@section editor-events Events

Everything that isn't a tile --- starts, enemies, pickups, springs, warps, tile behaviors --- is an event. Events sit on
tiles of the sprite layer, one per tile.

The <b>Events</b> tab lists them by category, with a search box. Clicking one chooses it with the event tool
(<kbd>Q</kbd>). Events the game doesn't use are found only by searching. The strip above the list holds favourite and
recent events.

@subsection editor-events-placing Placing and selecting

-   Click an empty tile to place the event, drag to keep placing. <kbd>Shift</kbd> places over existing events.
-   The <b>Mode</b> in the toolbar places by <b>Click</b>, in a <b>Rectangle</b>, or by <b>Fill</b> over the joined
    tiles of the same kind --- handy for vines, hooks and hurting tiles. The <b>Select</b> mode only selects.
-   Click an event to select it, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click to toggle it, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+drag to select a rectangle. Drag
    the selected events to move them. In the <b>Select</b> mode, a drag selects a rectangle, <kbd>Shift</kbd> adds and
    <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> removes.
-   The right button erases. <kbd>Alt</kbd>+click picks an event with all its parameters.
-   <kbd>Del</kbd>, cut, copy and paste work on the selected events. A warp copied with its target, or a trigger with
    its tiles, gets new identifiers each time it's pasted.
-   <b>Fill Selection with Event</b> in the <b>Select</b> menu places the event on the selected tiles that have none.

The right panel shows the parameters of the selected events, or of the event to place. A change applies to all
selected events of the same kind, and a value that differs among them is shown as <em>mixed</em>. Events of different
kinds share only their difficulties and flags. <b>Raw parameters</b> show all 16 bytes.

-   Some fields are shown only for some values of another field.
-   A crate or barrel edits the parameters of what is inside it.
-   A field that refers to another event: choose <b>Choose in the Events tab</b>, then click the event in the
    <b>Events</b> tab. <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels.
-   An <b>End of Level</b> whose next level is 0 shows where it leads by default.
-   A selected <b>Ambient Sound</b> plays its sound.

A single selected event has white <b>handles</b> for its values that are a place in the level --- the radius of a
light, the width of a bridge, the length of a chain, camera limits, the water level. A tube or a force has an arrow of
its speed. <kbd>Shift</kbd> snaps it to 8 directions. Dragging a handle is one undo step.

In the <b>Edit</b> menu, <b>Select Events of the Same Kind</b> selects all events like the selected one, and <b>Copy
Event to Every Tile Like Its Own</b> places it on every tile that shows the same tile --- place a hook once and copy it
to all hook tiles.

Two more tools place events:

-   <b>Measure</b> (<kbd>U</kbd>) --- drag to measure in pixels and tiles. It draws the arcs of jumps --- walking,
    dashing, the double jump and the copter --- and tells the easiest one that reaches. With <b>Stop at walls</b>, the
    arcs stop at walls. Started on a spring, it shows roughly where the spring throws the player.
-   <b>Events Along Path</b> (<kbd>K</kbd>) --- places the event evenly along a path, such as a row of coins. Click to
    add points, right-click to place them. Options: <b>Distance</b>, <b>Smooth</b>, <b>On tiles</b>.

@subsection editor-events-list Event list

<b>Event List</b> (<kbd>F3</kbd>) lists all events of the level, with their position, difficulties and main
parameters, filtered by what is typed. Click to select an event and move the view to it, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click or
<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click to select more and change them together.

@subsection editor-events-display How events look

Events look like the objects they create, with a corner mark in the color of their category. Others are boxes in that
color with their name. Badges show the difficulties (bottom right, if not all three), that it respawns (blue, top
right), and multiplayer only (purple, bottom left).

Arrows link warps to their targets (blue, a red cross means no target) and race waypoints in the order they're run
(green). A selected light shows its reach, a trigger the events it belongs with, a water level change the new level,
and a camera limit the area the camera can't reach. Events are dimmed while another layer is edited, and hidden under
other tools unless <b>Show Events</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>) is on.

@subsection editor-events-offgrid Off-grid events

Off-grid events are at any position, as levels made in MLLE can have them. They have a triangle in their corner, and
the game spawns them at their exact position.

-   <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click places an event off the grid.
-   They're selected, moved (<kbd>Shift</kbd> snaps to tile centers), erased and picked as other events.
-   <b>Snap Off-Grid Events to Grid</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu moves them onto their tiles.

@subsection editor-events-catalog Event catalog

The descriptions of events are compiled in, in @ref Editor::EventCatalog (`Editor/Events/EventCatalog.*.cpp`). They
describe the parameters as the game reads them. Each event has a type, a stable key, a name, a category, a preview
(@ref Editor::ResolveEventPreview()) and its parameters (@ref Editor::EventParamField) --- bits of the 16 bytes of
parameters, with how they're edited, when they're shown, what mirroring does to them, and their handles. Bytes no
parameter covers are kept as they are.

@subsection editor-events-extensions Events described outside the game

A level script can describe events the game doesn't know, one JSON object per line of its `.j2as` file:

@code{.cpp}
#pragma editor-event {"type": 300, "key": "MyThing", "name": "My Thing", "category": "enemy", "preview": {"metadata": "Enemy/Turtle", "animation": 1}, "params": [{"name": "Speed", "type": "uint", "offset": 0, "bits": 4, "max": 15, "default": 3}, {"name": "Mode", "type": "enum", "offset": 0, "shift": 4, "bits": 2, "options": ["Walk", "Fly", "Swim"]}]}
@endcode

Event packs for all levels are `.json` files in `Editor/Events/` of the content or the user content --- an event, an
array, or an object with an `"events"` array. The level script wins over event packs, and those over the built-in
events. They're read when a level is opened, and the script again whenever it's saved. <b>Read Event Descriptions
Again</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu reloads them. All fields are listed at @ref Editor::EventCatalog::LoadExtensions().
A description that can't be used is skipped and reported in <b>Check Level</b>.

@section editor-checking Checking the level

In the <b>Level</b> and <b>Show</b> menus:

-   <b>Statistics</b> --- counts for each difficulty: enemies, bosses, gems and coins (also those in crates), food,
    carrots, extra lives, ammo, checkpoints, warps, exits and starts. Click a count to select those events. Below, the
    results of the play-tests.
-   <b>Check Level</b> --- likely problems, see @ref editor-checking-check.
-   <b>Show Where the Player Can Get</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>) --- tints green every tile the
    player can reach from the start, by walking, jumping, swimming, climbing, springs, poles, tubes and warps. It errs
    on the safe side, so some routes may be missing. The <b>Show</b> menu chooses the character, and can add the
    multiplayer starts. Only in builds with multiplayer.
-   <b>Show Game Screen Frame</b> --- a frame of what the game shows: the game view, 4:3, 16:9, or half of a split
    screen.
-   <b>Compare with Saved</b> --- marks the tiles and events that differ from the saved file.

@subsection editor-checking-check Check Level

<b>Check Level</b> lists likely problems --- errors in red, then warnings and information, each kind with a button that
shows or hides it. The list follows the changes of the level. <b>Show</b> moves the view to a problem, and <b>Check
Again</b> checks everything again, including what the player can reach.

-   Starts --- no Jazz start, characters without a start of their own, no multiplayer start in a multiplayer level.
-   Warps without targets, targets nothing leads to, coin warps needing more coins than there are, missing texts,
    triggers that do nothing, parameters out of range, unknown events, layer effects that can't work.
-   No reachable exit, and pickups or race waypoints that may be out of reach.
-   Events inside solid tiles, next levels or music that don't exist, and more tile IDs than a script can use (4096).
-   Multiplayer --- fewer than 4 starts, bases for Capture the Flag missing or doubled, and gaps in the order of race
    waypoints.

Some problems have a button that fixes them: <b>Remove Warp</b>, <b>Add Warp Target</b>, <b>Add Start</b>, <b>Add
Multiplayer Start</b> and <b>Add CTF Base</b>.

@section editor-playing Playing the level

The level can be played right from the editor, saved or not. The view flies to the player and the game is revealed in
a growing ring, with a bar at the top to get back.

-   <b>Play</b> (<kbd>F5</kbd>) --- continues the last play-test where it was left. Tiles and events changed meanwhile
    are applied to it. Changes it can't take --- starts, warp targets, generators, events that change what a tile does,
    off-grid events, multiplayer, or another character, difficulty or game mode --- restart the level where the player
    was. Played alone at the same difficulty, the player keeps the lives, score and weapons, and what was collected or
    destroyed stays so.
-   <b>Play Here</b> (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>) --- starts at the pointer, or at the view center from the menu.
-   <b>Play from Start</b> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F5</kbd>).

The character, difficulty and game mode are chosen in the <b>Play</b> menu. <kbd>F5</kbd>, <b>Back to Editor</b> in the
pause menu, the exit or a game over return to the editor, with the view where the player was. Nothing is saved.
Finishing a level that has a next one offers <b>Edit It</b> (opens the next level in the editor), <b>Play On</b>
(plays the next level as the episode would) or <b>Stay Here</b>.

The <b>Play</b> menu can also play <b>local splitscreen multiplayer</b> --- Cooperation, Battle, Team Battle, Race,
Treasure Hunt or Capture the Flag, with 1 to 4 players --- to test multiplayer starts, laps and bases.

@subsection editor-playing-debug Debug toggles

In the <b>Debug</b> button of the bar while playing. The keys can be changed in <b>Keyboard Shortcuts</b>.

Key | Toggle
--- | ------
<kbd>F5</kbd> | <b>Back to Editor</b>, keeping the play-test to resume it
<kbd>F9</kbd> | <b>Slow Motion</b> --- half speed, quarter speed, normal
(none) | <b>Show Hitboxes</b> of players, enemies, collectibles and solid objects
(none) | <b>Show Event Zones</b> --- the tiles of modifiers, areas, triggers and warps
<kbd>F7</kbd> / <kbd>F8</kbd> | <b>Stop Time of Level</b> / <b>Next Frame</b>
<kbd>F6</kbd> | <b>Move Player to Pointer</b>

@subsection editor-playing-paths Paths of play-tests

After a play-test, the path of the player is drawn over the level --- thicker where faster, with red marks where it
died. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd> toggles it. The last 8 paths are kept with the level: <b>Show Deaths in
Play-Tests</b> shows where the player died in them, <b>Forget Paths of Play-Tests</b> removes them, and
<b>Statistics</b> tells the best time.

@section editor-files Files

-   <b>Save</b> --- writes the level. A level from @cpp "Cache/" @ce is saved as a new file instead, since the cache is
    made again from the original levels.
-   <b>Save As</b> --- saves into the user content (see @ref paths), or into @cpp "Content/" @ce if it can be written.
    The level script is copied along. If the level exists elsewhere too, the editor tells which copy the game loads.
-   <b>Export</b> --- saves for publishing, without what only the editor uses, after checking for problems such as a
    missing start. <b>Don't allow editing</b> marks the file so the editor won't open it. It's a courtesy, not a
    protection, so keep an editable copy.
-   <b>Export Package</b> --- a ZIP archive to publish, see @ref editor-files-package.

A failed save, such as on a full disk, never damages the file that was there.

@subsection editor-files-recovery Recovery

While a level has unsaved changes, a recovery copy is written every minute into @cpp "Editor/Recovery/" @ce in the
configuration directory, with the undo history. When a level with a newer copy is opened, the editor offers to restore
it. <b>Recovery Copies</b> in the <b>File</b> menu lists all copies, also of levels that were never saved.

@subsection editor-files-package Export Package

<b>Export Package</b> in the <b>File</b> menu writes a ZIP archive laid out as the content of the game, ready to be
unpacked into the user content:

-   `manifest.json` --- @cpp "format" @ce (1), @cpp "game" @ce, @cpp "name" @ce, @cpp "author" @ce,
    @cpp "version" @ce, @cpp "description" @ce, @cpp "tags" @ce, @cpp "levels" @ce, @cpp "episode" @ce and
    @cpp "files" @ce,
-   `preview.png`,
-   `Episodes/<episode>/<level>.j2l` --- exported, or as saved with <b>Keep what only the editor uses</b>, and with
    <b>All levels of the episode</b>, the other levels and the `.j2e` file too,
-   the scripts of the levels, the tile sets and music they use that aren't of the game, and the event packs.

Files that are needed but missing are listed in red.

@subsection editor-files-script Script, files changed elsewhere and the lock

-   <b>Edit Script</b> in the <b>Level</b> menu opens the script of the level (`<level>.j2as`) in the program the system
    has for it, or in the one named by @cpp "ScriptEditor" @ce in the settings (see @ref editor-files-settings). A level
    without a script gets one made from a template. Once the script is saved, the events it describes are read again.
-   A tile set changed by another program is noticed, and <b>Load Again</b> shows the change.
-   A level open in the editor has a lock file next to it, `<level>.j2l.lock`. Opening a level that another editor has
    open warns first.

@subsection editor-files-web In a browser

The web version can't open local files directly: <b>From This Computer</b> in the open dialog uploads a level, and
saving also downloads it. Settings and recovery copies are kept in the browser storage.

@subsection editor-files-settings Settings

The settings are stored in @cpp "Jazz2.Editor.json" @ce in the configuration directory (@ref Editor::EditorSettings).
The file can be edited by hand, best while the editor is closed. A value of a wrong type or out of range falls back to
the default.

Key | Meaning
--- | -------
@cpp "UiScale" @ce | Interface scale, @cpp 0 @ce by the window size
@cpp "Layout" @ce | @cpp 0 @ce by the window size, @cpp 1 @ce desktop, @cpp 2 @ce compact
@cpp "Expert" @ce, @cpp "ShowGrid" @ce, @cpp "PauseLayerMovement" @ce, @cpp "ReducedMotion" @ce | <b>Expert Mode</b>, <b>Show Grid</b>, <b>Pause Layer Movement</b>, <b>Reduce Motion</b>
@cpp "Theme" @ce | @cpp 0 @ce the usual theme, @cpp 1 @ce high contrast
@cpp "ScriptEditor" @ce | The program <b>Edit Script</b> opens scripts in, empty for the one the system has. A path, or a name found in `PATH`, with optional arguments where `{file}` stands for the script, otherwise it goes last. A path with spaces followed by arguments is in quotes. Set only in this file
@cpp "TileColumns" @ce | Tiles in a row of the tile picker, 1 to 32
@cpp "RightPanelWidth" @ce, @cpp "LayerListHeight" @ce, @cpp "PickerPanelWidth" @ce | Sizes of the panels
@cpp "PickerPanelPlacement" @ce, @cpp "RightPanelPlacement" @ce | Whether a panel floats and where: @cpp "Floating" @ce, @cpp "X" @ce, @cpp "Y" @ce, @cpp "Width" @ce, @cpp "Height" @ce
@cpp "PlayCharacter" @ce, @cpp "PlayDifficulty" @ce, @cpp "PlayGameMode" @ce, @cpp "PlayPlayerCount" @ce | How the level is played
@cpp "Tools" @ce | Tool options: @cpp "AcrossSeams" @ce, @cpp "IgnoreFlips" @ce, @cpp "OnlyEmpty" @ce, @cpp "WandGlobal" @ce, @cpp "Shape" @ce (@cpp 0 @ce line, @cpp 1 @ce rectangle, @cpp 2 @ce ellipse, @cpp 3 @ce filled ellipse), @cpp "EventMode" @ce (@cpp 0 @ce click, @cpp 1 @ce rectangle, @cpp 2 @ce fill), @cpp "MeasureArcs" @ce (bits: @cpp 1 @ce walk, @cpp 2 @ce dash, @cpp 4 @ce double jump, @cpp 8 @ce copter), @cpp "MeasureCollisions" @ce, @cpp "MirrorSwapTeams" @ce
@cpp "PlayTest" @ce | Play-test options: @cpp "TimeScale" @ce (0.05 to 4), @cpp "ShowHitboxes" @ce, @cpp "ShowEventZones" @ce, @cpp "ShowDeaths" @ce, @cpp "ReachCharacter" @ce (@cpp 0 @ce any, @cpp 1 @ce Jazz, @cpp 2 @ce Spaz, @cpp 3 @ce Lori), @cpp "ReachFromMpStarts" @ce
@cpp "ShowScreenFrame" @ce, @cpp "ScreenFrameKind" @ce | <b>Show Game Screen Frame</b>: @cpp 0 @ce game view, @cpp 1 @ce 4:3, @cpp 2 @ce 16:9, @cpp 3 @ce split screen
@cpp "CheckLevelFilter" @ce | What <b>Check Level</b> shows, bits: @cpp 1 @ce errors, @cpp 2 @ce warnings, @cpp 4 @ce information
@cpp "RecentEvents" @ce, @cpp "FavoriteEvents" @ce | Event types in the strip of the <b>Events</b> tab
@cpp "TileFavorites" @ce | An object by tile set name, each with @cpp "Recent" @ce and @cpp "Favorites" @ce tile indices
@cpp "RecentFiles" @ce | Files listed in the <b>File</b> menu, the most recent first

Where the view was in each level is stored in the level itself, in the @cpp "edoc" @ce chunk.

@section editor-formats Level and tile set formats

All reading and writing of levels --- by the converter, the game and the editor --- goes through
@ref Formats::LevelReader and @ref Formats::LevelWriter, and of tile sets through @ref Formats::TileSetFile.
@ref Formats::LevelFile describes the layout of a level.

The editor holds a level as an @ref Editor::LevelDocument. Loading and saving is lossless --- the file comes out byte
for byte the same. Tiles are referred to by tile set and index, so adding a tile set or an animated tile doesn't change
any layer. Every file is written to a temporary file first and replaces the target only once it's complete
(@ref Editor::AtomicFile).

@subsection editor-formats-chunks Chunks

A level can carry optional data at the end of its body, in chunks tagged by four characters. Readers skip chunks they
don't know. The case of the first two letters tells what a chunk is (@ref Formats::LevelFile):

-   First letter lowercase --- editor-only: the game never reads it, and export drops it.
-   Uppercase and lowercase, such as @cpp "Gscr" @ce --- <b>critical</b>: the level can't be played correctly without
    it, so a reader since 4.0.0 that doesn't know it refuses the level (@ref Formats::LevelFile::IsCriticalChunk()).
    Older readers skip it, so it may only hold data whose absence breaks nothing worse than the look of the level.
-   Both uppercase, such as @cpp "PRVW" @ce --- ancillary data of the game, skipped by a reader that doesn't know it.
    All chunks so far are ancillary or editor-only.

Tag | Contents
--- | --------
@cpp "PRVW" @ce | Preview for the custom level list, kept on export: @cpp u16 @ce width and height, then @cpp R, G, B @ce bytes row by row. The writer sets @ref LevelFlags::HasPreview with it
@cpp "SPRL" @ce | How the sprite layer is drawn, kept on export (@ref Formats::SpriteLayerChunk): @cpp u8 @ce renderer type and the 4 bytes of the color. Written only when it isn't drawn as plain tiles
@cpp "TXLP" @ce | Parameters of skies, tunnels, waves, cylinders and reflections their color has no room for, and textures of their own, kept on export (@ref Formats::TexturedLayerChunk): @cpp varuint @ce count, then for each layer @cpp varuint @ce index in layer order, @cpp varuint @ce size, @cpp f32 @ce fade X and Y (the amplitudes of a wave), @cpp u8 @ce fade and @cpp u8 @ce flags (1 spiral, 2 window of the layer, 4 the layer, 8 half size, 16 swapped angles of a wave, 32 the perspective of JJ2+). A layer with inner speeds or its own texture goes on with @cpp f32 @ce inner speed X and Y, inner auto speed X and Y and @cpp u8 @ce texture (0 the tiles, 1 an image of 256×256 palette indices, which follows). Written only for layers without the defaults
@cpp "LTXT" @ce | Translations of the level texts, kept on export (@ref Formats::LevelTextsChunk): @cpp u8 @ce version (1), @cpp varuint @ce number of languages, then each as a @cpp varuint @ce size and a record of @cpp str8 @ce language, @cpp varuint @ce number of texts, and for each @cpp varuint @ce text number, @cpp varuint @ce length and UTF-8 text
@cpp "LNAM" @ce | Layer names, kept on export (@ref Formats::LayerNamesChunk): one @cpp str8 @ce per layer, in layer order. Written only if a layer has a name
@cpp "LNUM" @ce | Numbers of the layers in the original game, kept on export (@ref Formats::LayerNumbersChunk): one @cpp u8 @ce per layer, in layer order, 1 to 8 for the layers of a level of the original game, 0 for the others. Written only if a layer has a number
@cpp "ela2" @ce | Layer editor state (@ref Editor::LevelDocument::LayerStateChunk): @cpp u8 @ce version (1), @cpp varuint @ce number of layers, @cpp varuint @ce bytes of each layer, then those bytes of each layer --- hidden and locked flags, and the editor opacity if any layer has one
@cpp "ann2" @ce | Bookmarks and notes (@ref Editor::Annotation): @cpp u8 @ce version (2), @cpp varuint @ce count, then each as a @cpp varuint @ce size and a record of @cpp i32 @ce X and Y in pixels, @cpp u8 @ce bookmark number (0 = note), @cpp varuint @ce length and text. Version 2 adds @cpp u32 @ce color, the author, @cpp i64 @ce time, @cpp u8 @ce flags (1 = done) and the replies, each with its author, time and text
@cpp "spl2" @ce | Ground lines (@ref Editor::TerrainSpline): @cpp u8 @ce version (1), @cpp varuint @ce count, then each as a @cpp varuint @ce size and a record of @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce depth, @cpp u8 @ce kind (ground, ceiling, band, shape), @cpp u8 @ce flags (1 = smooth), @cpp varuint @ce point count and @cpp f32 @ce X and Y of each in layer pixels, @cpp varuint @ce count of written tiles and for each @cpp i32 @ce X and Y, then the written and previous tile, each as @cpp u16 @ce index, @cpp u8 @ce source and @cpp u8 @ce flags
@cpp "edoc" @ce | View state, written on every save so the level opens as it was left (@ref Editor::DocumentViewState): @cpp u8 @ce version (1), @cpp u16 @ce flags (camera, grid, events, play path, flat layers, play settings --- 0x40 is reserved), @cpp f32 @ce center X, Y and zoom relative to the game's, @cpp i32 @ce layer, @cpp u8 @ce mask mode and picker panel, @cpp u8 @ce character, difficulty, game mode and players, two reserved @cpp i32 @ce written as 0
@cpp "ppth" @ce | Paths of the last play-tests (@ref Editor::PlayPathHistory): @cpp u8 @ce version (2), then up to 8 paths, each with its time, whether the level was finished, and up to 4096 points, each as a difference from the previous one with the speed of the player there (a @cpp varuint @ce in 1/16 px per frame, up to 1024 px per frame)
@cpp "pkgi" @ce | What <b>Export Package</b> says of the package: @cpp u8 @ce version (1), then a JSON object
@cpp "rcov" @ce | Path of the level a recovery copy belongs to (recovery copies only)
@cpp "rhst" @ce | History of changes of a recovery copy (@ref Editor::CommandArchive): @cpp u8 @ce version (3), a fingerprint of the layout of the records and of how snapshots are written, @cpp varuint @ce number of commands, of those done and of those done when it was saved plus one, then each command as a @cpp varuint @ce size and its data, in the native byte order (recovery copies only)

A versioned chunk is read for any version but 0. A newer version only adds fields at the end of a record, which a
reader skips. @cpp "edoc" @ce, @cpp "PRVW" @ce, @cpp "ppth" @ce, @cpp "rcov" @ce and @cpp "rhst" @ce are made anew
by the editor, so undo never brings them back.

@subsection editor-formats-compat Compatibility with 3.9.0

Levels move between this version and 3.9.0, with these differences:

-   The editor chunks of 3.9.0 (@cpp "anno" @ce, @cpp "spln" @ce, @cpp "elay" @ce) had no version. They're still
    read, but only the versioned ones are written, so 3.9.0 opens a level saved since without its notes, ground lines
    and layer flags. A level that has both was saved by 3.9.0 last, which keeps the versioned ones as they were, so the
    legacy ones are taken then (with the colors, authors and replies of the notes that are still the same, and the
    editor opacity of the layers, which the legacy chunks have no room for).
-   <b>Speed from level start</b> is placed as <b>Speed</b>, and <b>Inherent offset</b> moves any layer that has it.
-   A cylinder or a reflection is drawn as tiles, a tunnel centered, a palette-shifted layer as an ordinary one tinted
    by its color, and the sprite layer always as plain tiles.
-   A level saved by the editor of 3.9.0 shows no preview in the level list until it's saved again.
-   The editor of 3.9.0 doesn't know the layer names (@cpp "LNAM" @ce) and the numbers of the layers in the original
    game (@cpp "LNUM" @ce), so it shows the layers named by what they are. It keeps both chunks as they are, even when
    it moves the layers, so they can belong to other layers then.

@subsection editor-formats-limits Limits and checks

Levels and tile sets can come from anywhere, so the readers check everything before relying on it, and refuse what no
editor would write:

-   A layer of more than @ref Formats::LevelFile::MaxLayerSize "65535" tiles on a side, more than
    @ref Formats::LevelFile::MaxLayerTiles "2²⁶" tiles in a layer or @ref Formats::LevelFile::MaxTotalLayerTiles "2²⁷"
    in all layers, a decompressed body over @ref Formats::LevelFile::MaxBodySize "1 GiB", an embedded tile set or a
    chunk over @ref Formats::LevelFile::MaxBlobSize "256 MiB", more than 65535 off-grid events, 65536 overridden tiles
    or masks, or 65535 chunks, and a @cpp varuint @ce of more than 5 bytes or a value over 32 bits.
-   Offsets and speeds of a layer, and parameters of a textured layer, that aren't finite are replaced by their
    defaults when a level is read or written, the level itself is still read.
-   Flag bits that would change the layout of a record (those of an extra tile set outside of 0x07) and a layer kind
    above the sprite layer (2) make a level unreadable since 4.0.0, instead of misread. The reserved event flag 0x08,
    which 3.9.0 could write, is dropped instead.
-   A tile set path must be relative and must not go up, and no part of it may end with a dot or a space or be a
    device name of Windows (@ref Formats::LevelFile::IsSafeTileSetPath()).
-   A tile set has one or four channels, a whole number of tiles at most 32768 pixels wide, at least one tile, and a
    body that fits the file (@ref Formats::TileSetFile::Validate()).
-   A savegame of a level whose size changed since is refused, instead of restored into the wrong places.

@ref LevelFlags::NotEditable is a courtesy to the author, a single bit anyone can clear, not a protection.

@subsection editor-formats-verify Verifying the formats

The `verify-formats` command of @ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" reads every level and tile set in a directory and
writes it back into memory, which must give the same file byte for byte (or the same content, for files written by
other tools). With `--sweep-truncated`, every file is also read cut short at many places, which must never pass for a
different level.
`fuzz-formats` tests the readers with mutated files, and `level-to-text` writes a level as text for version control
(see @ref asset-packer-usage).

@code{.sh}
AssetPacker verify-formats <directory> [--sweep-truncated]
AssetPacker fuzz-formats <directory> [<findings directory>] [--iterations=N] [--seed=N]
AssetPacker level-to-text <level.j2l> <level.txt>
AssetPacker text-to-level <level.txt> <level.j2l>
@endcode

@section editor-testing Testing the editor

Debug builds have an editor self-test. `/editor-selftest` loads every level in @cpp "Cache/Episodes/" @ce (or a given
directory) into an @ref Editor::LevelDocument and checks that saving gives the same file, that editor data survives
saving and loading while export drops it, that random changes undone and redone give the original and the changed
level, and that <b>Check Level</b> runs. It also validates the @ref editor-events-catalog "event catalog" and tests
the parts of the editor on their own --- from the PNG codec and the quantizer to the history, recovery copies, the tools
and the shortcuts. Results go to the log, then the game quits.

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /editor-selftest
Jazz2 /editor-selftest --unit
@endcode

With `--unit` only the tests that need no game data run, so no converted game is needed: the tools are driven through
a host of their own over a document (`Editor/Tests/FakeToolHost.h`), and the level shown is checked to follow the
document by its layers only. `Sources/Jazz2/Editor/Tests/CheckIncludes.ps1` checks that the folders of the editor
include each other only in the allowed directions, CI runs it before the build.

Debug builds also run `/console` commands once the editor is open, in order, so it can be driven without input ---
such as to take screenshots. Positions are in window pixels.

Command | What it does
------- | ------------
`/jump <x> <y> [scale]`, `/fly <x> <y> <scale>` | Move the camera at once, or fly it there
`/zoom <scale>`, `/fit` | Zoom, fit the whole level
`/view <x> <y> <percent>` | Center the view at a position (`c c` for the middle of the level) at a zoom as the status bar shows it
`/drift <dx> <dy> [zoom] [frames]` | Move the view by so many screen pixels every frame, zooming out by the factor and back every so many frames. `/drift 0 0` stops it
`/lod <on\|off\|auto\|stats>` | Draw the layers as cached images always, never, or by the zoom. `stats` logs the pixels per tile, wanted and used, and the memory
`/perf reset`, `/perf log <label>` | Start averaging the frame statistics, log them
`/layer <index>`, `/hide <index>`, `/layers` | Edit a layer, hide it, log the layers
`/paint <layer> <x> <y> <[source:]tile> [width] [height]` | Paint tiles of the default tile set, or of a source numbered as in `/addtiles`
`/undo`, `/redo`, `/action <id>` | Undo, redo, run a menu action
`/click <x> <y>`, `/rclick <x> <y>`, `/drag <x1> <y1> <x2> <y2>`, `/hover <x> <y>`, `/wheel <x> <y> <amount>` | Pointer input
`/press <x> <y>`, `/move <x> <y>`, `/release <x> <y>` | A drag over several commands
`/key <name>` | Press `enter`, `escape`, `delete`, `f5`, `left`, `right`, `up`, `down`, `space`, `ctrl`, `shift` or `alt`
`/keydown <name>`, `/keyup <name>` | Hold or release a key
`/type <text>` | Type into what has the keyboard focus, a space written as `+`
`/focus [back]` | Move the keyboard focus as <kbd>Tab</kbd> or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> does
`/gamepad <x> <y>`, `/radial [<x> <y>]` | Use the gamepad with its cursor at a position. Show the tool ring, or point at a tool of it
`/touch <down\|pointerdown\|move\|pointerup\|up> <id> <x> <y> ...` | A touch event in screen pixels, the finger that came or went given last
`/keyboard` | Show the editor's own on-screen keyboard
`/theme <default\|contrast> [still]` | Apply a theme, with reduced motion, not saved
`/safearea <left> <top> <right> <bottom>` | Set the Safe Area of the game in percent, as its graphics options do
`/keymap <default\|jcs\|mlle>` | Apply a set of shortcuts and save it
`/renderer <type> [red [alpha]]` | Set the renderer of the current layer, see @ref Tiles::LayerRendererType (10 sky, 11 tunnel, 14 wave, 15 cylinder, 16 reflection), and optionally its color
`/textured <x> <y> [fade [flags]]` | Set the parameters of a tunnel, cylinder or reflection of the current layer that its color has no room for (@ref Formats::TexturedLayerParams)
`/innerspeeds <x> <y> <auto x> <auto y>` | Set the inner speeds of a reflection drawn only where the layer is
`/addtiles <source> <image.png> [all] [dither]` | <b>Add Tiles from Image</b>. `all` keeps duplicates. Source is a number as in `/applytiles`, `level`, or `new:<name>`
`/fxtiles <source> <kind> <width> <height>` | <b>Generate Effect Tiles</b> into a source as in `/addtiles`. Kind is an @ref Editor::FxTileKind
`/newtileset <name> <tiles> [extra\|default\|save]` | <b>New Tile Set</b>
`/importtileset <image.png> <name> [extra\|default\|save] [own\|true\|match\|reduce] [dither]` | <b>New Tile Set from Image</b>, colors kept by default
`/exporttileset <image.png>` | <b>Export Tile Set Image</b>
`/applytiles <source> [new name\|-] [palette]` | <b>Save Tile Changes into Tile Set</b>. Source 0 is the default tile set, 1+ the extra ones. `palette` saves the level palette too
`/tileoverride <tile ID> <index>` | Fill a tile with one palette entry and make it solid, in this level only
`/palette <entry> <rrggbb>` | Set an entry of the level palette, and log how long it took
`/palettemove <first> <last> <-1\|1> [keep]` | Move palette entries, with `keep` keeping the look of the level, and log how long it took
`/animframe <index> <frames>` | Set the frame time of an animated tile
`/placeevent <key> <x> <y> [bytes...]` | Place an event of the catalog on a tile, the bytes given replacing the first bytes of its parameters
`/placeoffgrid <key> <x> <y> [delay]` | Place an off-grid event at a position in pixels, or with a delay in seconds, as a generator
`/brush <key>`, `/selectevent <x> <y> [add]`, `/events` | Choose the event to place, select (or add) the event of a tile, log the selection and the event tool
`/texts`, `/findevents <key>` | Log the level texts, or up to 20 events of a kind
`/links [next] [secret] [bonus]` | Set the next, secret and bonus level (`-` for none), then log them. Without arguments only log them
`/check [index]` | Log the problems <b>Check Level</b> finds, or fix the one of the index
`/saveprefab <name>`, `/exportrules <tile set>...` | Save the brush as a prefab. Make rules of tile sets from what the levels use the most (a space in a name written as `+`)
`/pngtest <directory>` | Read every PNG in a directory, compare with a same-named `.rgba` file if present, write it to `out/` and read it back
`/preview <image.png>` | Write the preview the level would be saved with
`/save <path>`, `/export <path>`, `/exportplayonly <path>`, `/autosave` | Save, export, export as not editable, write a recovery copy
`/restorecopy <path>` | Restore a recovery copy with its history
`/rebuild` | Load the shown level again from the document
`/playring <progress>` | Freeze the play transition at a point between 0 and 1
`/stopplay [keep]` | Return from the play-test to the editor, with `keep` keeping it to resume
`/exitlevel` | Leave the played level through its exit
`/teleport <x> <y>`, `/step` | Move the player of the play-test to a position. Go on by one update
`/playerstate [<score> <lives>]` | Add score and lives to the player of the play-test, and log what it has
`/paths`, `/reach [character] [mp starts]` | Log the kept paths of play-tests, or how many tiles can be reached
`/wait <frames>`, `/quit` | Wait before the next commands, quit

@code{.sh}
Jazz2 /edit prince/01_castle1 /console "/jump 320 320 2" /console "/paint 2 8 8 5 6 4" /console "/wait 30" /console "/quit"
@endcode

*/

}
